\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces The candidate-set sweep at 113.47M. Each row is a separate fine-tune of the same pretrained checkpoint, at the suite's frozen recipe (4{,}000 stratified rows, 3{,}000 steps, batch 32, LR $10^{-4}$, seed 0), differing only in the number of candidates $|C|$ packed into every training and test row. Test rows are the same 2{,}000 rows at every width. A fixed model restricted to a sub-bank is not an experiment: with the gold always present, deleting candidates can only remove wrong argmaxes, so restricted accuracy is guaranteed to be at least the full-bank accuracy. Width is therefore trained, not simulated. Wilson intervals are 95\%; NLL is over the sampled candidate set and is not comparable across widths. Read from \texttt  {results/e4\_candidate\_sweep/SUMMARY.json}; \texttt  {scripts/e4\_summary.py} verifies every per-example record before writing it.\relax }}{28}{table.caption.19}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e4}{{4.9}{28}{The candidate-set sweep at 113.47M. Each row is a separate fine-tune of the same pretrained checkpoint, at the suite's frozen recipe (4{,}000 stratified rows, 3{,}000 steps, batch 32, LR $10^{-4}$, seed 0), differing only in the number of candidates $|C|$ packed into every training and test row. Test rows are the same 2{,}000 rows at every width. A fixed model restricted to a sub-bank is not an experiment: with the gold always present, deleting candidates can only remove wrong argmaxes, so restricted accuracy is guaranteed to be at least the full-bank accuracy. Width is therefore trained, not simulated. Wilson intervals are 95\%; NLL is over the sampled candidate set and is not comparable across widths. Read from \texttt {results/e4\_candidate\_sweep/SUMMARY.json}; \texttt {scripts/e4\_summary.py} verifies every per-example record before writing it.\relax }{table.caption.19}{}}
\newlabel{tab:e4@cref}{{[table][9][4]4.9}{[1][27][]28}}
